[Music]  hello and welcome now every single one  of us has a responsibility to tackle the  climate emergency businesses and  governments have a significant role to  play in driving the positive change we  need  and as they look to make that change  happen they may find answers in some  unexpected places  we're talking today about what  businesses can learn from space and why  the space race may provide important  lessons for the race to net zero  joining me to discuss this are zubin  randaria esg leader at pwc uk  tania sinclair policy director uk and  ireland at chargepoint and libby jackson  human exploration program manager at the  uk space agency  well it's great to have you all here  today and i wanted to ask you about  the 1960s with the space race and how it  relates to us today in tackling climate  change and also the race to net zero  let's start off with you  when you look back the space race the  late 1950s 1960s were an amazing time in  space exploration  the world went from having never put  anything into space in the late 1950s  the first satellite flew in 1957 to  first human in space in 1961.  less than a decade later we've gone from  that very first human space flight to  landing humans on the moon  and in order to do that we had to  develop an entire industry we had to  work out how to take computers from the  size of rooms and shrink them into  shoeboxes we had to work out how you  could communicate with people in space  how you could keep them alive in space  and all of that was done in a background  of two countries competing  to prove that they were better than the  other it was the united states and the  soviet union  but that race has led to an industry  that we all rely on every single day  today we have seen innovations come into  our everyday lives in form  of materials uh in in applications here  we have loads and loads of things that  come from the space industry and today  it's in an industry of collaboration  yeah certainly in my industry in  electric car charging the parallels are  really clear actually even though there  are decades between the space race and  what we're doing today you know talking  about starting an industry from scratch  15 years ago there weren't really  electric cars and there was no electric  car charging industry and now there are  dozens of companies so many of them here  in the uk who are scale-ups and really  kind of growing very quickly all with  the ambition to provide enough charging  infrastructure so that people feel ready  to get into electric cars and why is  that so important because we have a net  zero  target to meet and a huge part of that  is the decarbonization of transport so  it's really important that we take those  lessons and we understand how to do  things quickly innovate go from uh  design to commercialization really  really quickly because we don't have the  luxury of time when it comes to dealing  with net zero  we talk about a challenge and the the  space race was a phenomenal challenge  wasn't it in 10 years what was achieved  but when we think about net zero we've  got 10 years  to halve global emissions to reduce  global warming by one and a half degrees  otherwise the scientists tell us there  will be pretty bad implications for the  planet and therefore whilst the space  race was a race between two superpowers  for national prestige  this is a challenge on a completely  different order this is a planetary  challenge this isn't one that can be  restricted to individual  companies organizations countries this  is one where the planet needs to solve  it together olivia you talked about  america and at the time  of the space phase president kennedy he  said he wanted to put a man on the moon  and he publicly stated that do you think  that was what made it happen and is that  where we're going with net zero i think  when you look back in history what  happened that there were various things  there was that um challenge and when  when kennedy said that  you have to remember the americans had  one human um alan shepard had been in  space for 15 minutes it was crazy to  think that that could be done yeah i  think the question of political will is  a really interesting one because  certainly in my field in the mobility  and decarbonization of transport you  have a lot of politicians all over the  world making really big commitments that  they want to see the end of petrol and  diesel vehicles you know here in the uk  the date is 20 30 after which you won't  be able to buy a new petrol or diesel  car or van  however there's  really noble intentions that politicians  set those targets for but it almost kind  of takes us off the hook in a way  because you know like you were saying  that there's a lot of uh individual  responsibility when it comes to  achieving that zero we all have our role  to play and so  politicians can set targets but you know  we all have to be collectively part of  the the effort to meet them the the key  thing is to follow through and execute  on those commitments and not all of the  solutions are there there needs to be  further innovations for example to  deliver on what needs to happen which we  don't know today a bit like when kennedy  made that statement necessity is the  mother of invention we saw it then we  we've seen it now and we will see it  with with the race to net zero if  everybody um comes together well i think  innovation is a key word here because  the space race was a breakthrough for  innovation do you think  it should be a key word for net zero  yes absolutely although innovation needs  skills it needs the pipeline of people  and ideas going into it in order to  develop that innovation especially here  in the uk you know the good thing about  ideas is that they can be global but you  know here we have a real opportunity for  kids who are in school now to be able to  be inspired by the challenge of net zero  and really go into careers that you know  can practically deliver that innovation  uh so we're there by 2050. so i think  there's been a phenomenal amount of  innovation you know if you only look  back  seven eight years 2013 there's about  half a billion dollars of investment in  climate tech from the venture capital  industry one ford six years and the 16  billion dollars now that's a rate of  growth three times as much as has gone  into artificial intelligence which we  see all around us  but when you look in aggregate it's  still a drop in the ocean compared with  the innovation that's needed to solve  such a global problem and we've seen so  much progression you know in ev charging  for cars you know and solar charging and  stuff like that but there's still so  much more that needs to be done that we  don't know today and we need to solve a  very big problem together collectively  and collaborate and therefore that's  going to drive a significant amount of  innovation going forward so i suppose  it's about greater collaboration the  communication between both sides i think  this is one area where business can  really learn from not just space but  science you know science is based upon  you know building upon the shoulders of  others what has gone before and for  business  it by and large it's a tendency to  compete to do things proprietary to keep  things to yourself to create value for  your own organization shareholders  here is a problem which can only be sold  if organizations governance governments  non-governmental organizations  collaborate to solve  a planetary problem this isn't a  shareholder problem or a company problem  and therefore this is one thing where  this will only get solved through  collaboration  yeah i agree with that but there's also  a really interesting other angle to that  which is the competition also can  accelerate the pace of change so you see  it in the car industry at the moment a  lot of car companies really driven by  tesla at the forefront are in a race  competing with one another to deliver  electric cars and vans and trucks as  quickly as possible and that competition  is really helping move our market  forward i think more quickly than it  otherwise would have but yes there's  also examples of collaboration going on  in that space because a lot of those  companies are working together  in a way they've never done before  whether it's on battery technology or  software to actually bring those cars  that might have different badges on them  but actually have the same technology  underneath a bit that goes back to what  you're saying about russia and america i  was about to say you bring it together  now that the the whole space industry  grew and the technology that we have  grew out of the second world war the  rockets that we all rely on to put all  these satellites and spacecraft in space  that we all rely on every single day  that technology came out of the second  world war you then had the cold war and  america and the soviet union you know  racing each other to get to the moon  but after that  they came together they opened up to the  rest of the world and we built the  international space station and that has  been a collaboration of america canada  russia japan countries across europe  today including the uk  um working together to build this  amazing unique laboratory so where does  all this investment come from how do we  pay for this  it's a really interesting question  because there is a significant amount of  capital around the world that is looking  for a home and as we think about the  shift in capital as investors big  pension funds insurance companies  sovereign wealth funds are looking to  deploy their their capital to inform  increasingly they're looking for clean  responsible  well-run companies and we see that's the  opportunity if you can organize your  business so that you can demonstrate how  clean it is then you're going to attract  capital it's interesting in just in the  uk  there's long-term investors who invest  in infrastructure and the uk needs about  40 billion a year invested in  low-carbon digital infrastructure about  half of that  is accessible to pension funds people  who are very patient who can invest for  the long term but half of that is high  risk stuff the big challenge then is to  de-risk that investment to develop it  out of the lab commercialize it and take  it into what we see in for example  electric vehicles today rather than 15  years ago then it's available available  to be investible by long-term capital  yeah in the electric vehicle sector the  assumption is that governments have to  seed or at least start to fund the  sector in order to make it sustainable  over the last decade the government has  funded uh every new purchase of an  electric car  from it used to be five thousand pounds  now it's around two thousand pounds and  that's not necessary anymore uh because  these businesses are growing and they're  sustainable in their own right the  company that i work for went public this  year listed on the new york stock  exchange you know there's a demand and  an interest in wanting to invest in  these kinds of companies and then drive  the technology forward that way  so how do you bring people along  into this race to net zero how do you  inspire them to think yeah we're going  to jump on this bandwagon that's  something where i think space and human  space flight plays its part uh in the  astronauts always come back from space  and are able to share first-hand seeing  our planet our beautiful very delicate  planet in this amazing blackness of  space with this thin teeny tiny delicate  atmosphere and it's stories like that i  think that open people's eyes where we  have a part to play in in highlighting  how important our planet is how unique  it is in the as far as we know entire  universe at the minute and so that's  somewhere where i think space can be  there we also play our part in in  looking at how all this is going to  happen to monitor the earth to make sure  that we are understanding all of these  things we have to have data and of all  the data that we need half of it can  only be got from space i think this is a  really interesting one because in  business it's not one where business has  to convince  our people to come along it's working  the other way around you know it's our  people who are saying you know this is  the world that i want to live in an  environment i want to live in a planet i  want to live in a society i want to live  in and  people wanting business to catch up and  i think there's a lot of pressure from  younger people in particular in terms of  the organizations that they want to work  for the values that they associate with  those organizations and selecting those  organizations that they want to  participate in their careers with i  think this ties in nicely with the word  legacy here because the space race it  created a legacy it had a massive impact  do you think there's going to be a huge  legacy with net zero for our children  it's it's  our job  um to leave a an or a planet that is fit  for our children  and we have a very very short space of  time it feels like a long time people  talk about 2050 2050 is going to be with  us quite soon we've got 10 years to half  global admissions and 10 years was the  time which we're amazed that we sent a  man to the moon i think that if we can  talk to nets talk to our our next  generation about net zero being a  problem of the present even though we've  set ourselves a nice convenient far away  target then it's really something that  kind of will help the next generation  want to solve a problem because it's a  problem of today and it's a problem that  will be in our lifetimes but very much  part of theirs as well well this has  been a brilliant and wide-ranging  conversation and i've enjoyed every  minute of it i really want to thank our  panelists for taking part in sharing so  much insight into a topic that will  shape all of our 